{
 "language_code": "eng",
 "language_probability": 0.9916513562202454,
 "text": "For about 17 centuries, Hebrew was no one's mother tongue. Jews read and wrote it, but many modern things had only phrases for names. Even a dictionary was called Sefer Milim, a copy of German Wörterbuch. In 1880, Eliezer Ben-Yehuda wrote that such copies had made Hebrew almost German. Instead, he made Milon from milah, which means word. The letter mem can mark a tool, so he put it before the root for sling and made makleht ah, a cannon. In his own dictionary, this sign marks words he coined that people had already accepted. It stands before the bicycle, a word for two wheels modeled on the word for ears. No one is sure what the prophet Ezekiel meant by hashmal. An ancient Greek translation gave it as electron, so a poet used it for electricity. Ben-Yehuda did not mark that word as his own. Yechiel Michael Pines apparently named the tomato agvaniyah, from a root that means desire. Ben-Yehuda found the word immodest and kept it out of his dictionary. Today, Hebrew speakers call the tomato by the word he kept out. [gentle music]",
